WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.312
This query filters sales before returning results.

00:00:03.312 --> 00:00:10.287
In Spark, filters can be especially important because they may reduce how much data needs to be processed.

00:00:10.287 --> 00:00:14.302
When data is stored in an efficient format and partitioned well,

00:00:14.302 --> 00:00:20.167
some filters can help avoid scanning unnecessary data.

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.182
The teaching habit is simple: filter as early as the logic allows.

00:00:23.182 --> 00:00:30.172
Connect this idea to a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions.

00:00:30.172 --> 00:00:35.982
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:35.982 --> 00:00:43.372
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:43.372 --> 00:00:49.147
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:49.147 --> 00:00:55.030
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
